Title: an evicted tenant context leaks its conductor ledger handle
Created: 2026-09-02
Author: MinhHaDuong

--- log ---
2026-09-02T18:52Z MinhHaDuong created

--- body ---
## Context

Found by the review of the fork's merge commit `c031478`, alongside 0594 and
0596. Low severity, and the honest framing is that the merge *widened* a
pre-existing omission rather than creating one.

`ContextCache` keeps one `ToolContext` per authenticated Zotero user and caps
the map at `maxEntries` (50). Eviction is the whole of this:

```ts
private evictIfNeeded(): void {
  while (this.entries.size > this.maxEntries) {
    ...
    this.entries.delete(oldestKey);     // and nothing else
  }
}
```

The two SQLite handles that context owns — `c.search` (v1, pre-existing) and
now `c.conductorLedger` (v2, added by this merge) — are never closed. Closing
is what checkpoints the write-ahead log and releases the file handle, and the
only place that does it is `flushIndexes`, which runs from the shutdown
handler and walks `this.entries.values()` — the *live* entries. An evicted
tenant is no longer in that map, so its handles are closed by nothing, ever:
they survive until the process exits, holding an open fd and an
uncheckpointed WAL each.

So a server that has served more than 50 distinct Zotero users accumulates
two stranded handles per tenant beyond the cap, and the uncheckpointed WAL is
the more annoying half — the next startup has to replay it.

## Why this is not a one-line fix

The class docstring states the reason eviction does not close:

> Eviction only drops the cache entry; live sessions keep the ctx they
> already closed over.

That is real. `resolve()` hands the `ToolContext` out, and an in-flight
session holds it directly rather than looking it up again — so closing the
ledger at eviction would pull the file out from under a request that is
still running, turning a slow leak into a live error. The fix is therefore
about *lifetime*, not about adding a `close()` call:

- refcount the context (a session checks out and returns it) and close on
  the last release; or
- close on eviction only when nothing holds it, and otherwise hand it to a
  reaper that closes it when the last holder is gone; or
- decide the cap is the wrong mechanism for a resource with a lifetime, and
  evict on idleness with a grace period longer than any request.

Any of the three is a design decision, which is why this is a ticket rather
than a patch appended to a bug-fix PR.

## Reproduction

By inspection rather than by measurement, and the inspection is short:

1. `src/server.ts`, `ContextCache.evictIfNeeded` — `this.entries.delete(...)`
   with no `close()` on either handle.
2. `src/server.ts`, `ContextCache.flushIndexes` — iterates
   `[this.operatorCtx, ...this.entries.values()]`, so it can only reach what
   is still cached.
3. `src/server.ts` around the context build — each per-user context opens its
   own `conductorLedger` through `openConductorLedger`, so the handle is per
   tenant, not shared.

A measured reproduction would need 51 authenticated tenants against one
server and an fd count, which is worth building only once the lifetime
question above is answered — the count is the test for whichever answer
wins.

## Relevant files

- Fork `src/server.ts` — `ContextCache`: `resolve`, `evictIfNeeded`,
  `flushIndexes`, and the class docstring quoted above.
- Fork `src/features/search/conductor/store.ts` — `openConductorLedger`, the
  per-tenant open.

## Actions

1. Pick a lifetime rule for a cached `ToolContext` from the three above (or a
   fourth), and write it into the class docstring, which currently documents
   the symptom as if it were the design.
2. Implement it for both handles at once — `search` and `conductorLedger`
   have the same lifetime and fixing only the newer one would leave the
   older leak with a test that looks like coverage.

## Test

- An eviction under the chosen rule closes both handles: assert on the
  handles themselves (a closed `DatabaseSync` throws on use) rather than on
  an fd count, which is portable and exact.
- A context still held by an in-flight caller is *not* closed by an eviction
  that happens during that call — the property the current code protects and
  the fix must not lose.

## Verification

- [ ] Both handles of an evicted context are closed, on whatever schedule
      the chosen rule sets
- [ ] An in-flight session's context survives its own eviction
- [ ] The shutdown path still closes every live context, as it does today

## Invariants

- No change to what `resolve()` returns for a given user, cached or not.
- Upstream tests green.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] A cached tenant context has one stated lifetime and both its SQLite
      handles follow it, merged on a fork branch, tests green
